res regression model. The values of all covariates (except gender) were allowed to with follow-up time. change","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":515.046182142629,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":35.0,"r":550.3333333333334,"t":539.6666666666666},"charspan":[0,166],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/5","text":"All variables simultaneously entered in a repeated measures regression model. The values of all covariates (except gender) were allowed to with follow-up time. change"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"footnote","orig":"All variables simultaneously entered in multiple linear regression model.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":536.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":48.333333333333336,"r":353.0,"t":548.6666666666666},"charspan":[0,73],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/6","text":"All variables simultaneously entered in multiple linear regression model."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"For the composite outcome of death or ESRD (Figure 3c), a U-shaped relationship between Sx and the composite outcome was evident. There was a significantly higher hazard for death / ESRD at lower serum SK' the significance of which was attenuated by adjustment for serum albumin in the model. As remained significant predictor of the composite outcome before and after adjustment for serum albumin (Table 3) Higher Sx was not significantly associated with the composite outcome","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":378.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":33.0,"r":290.0,"t":487.3333333333333},"charspan":[0,477],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/7","text":"For the composite outcome of death or ESRD (Figure 3c), a U-shaped relationship between Sx and the composite outcome was evident. There was a significantly higher hazard for death / ESRD at lower serum SK' the significance of which was attenuated by adjustment for serum albumin in the model. As remained significant predictor of the composite outcome before and after adjustment for serum albumin (Table 3) Higher Sx was not significantly associated with the composite outcome"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"A total of 190 cardiovascular events (70 coronary , 50 cerebrovascular, and 70 peripheral vascular) that required hospitaliza tion were documented the course of the study. A significant quadratic relationship was seen between SK' and the relative hazard of first cardiovascular event or death (Figure 3d) was noted. Unlike overall mortality , this was statistically significant for Sk >55 mmol/L but not for lower potassium (<4.0 mmol/L) After addition of serum albumin to the model , higher Sx but not lower Sx was associated with the composite outcome (Table 3) during","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":260.33333333333337,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":33.0,"r":289.0,"t":379.3333333333333},"charspan":[0,570],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/8","text":"A total of 190 cardiovascular events (70 coronary , 50 cerebrovascular, and 70 peripheral vascular) that required hospitaliza tion were documented the course of the study. A significant quadratic relationship was seen between SK' and the relative hazard of first cardiovascular event or death (Figure 3d) was noted. Unlike overall mortality , this was statistically significant for Sk >55 mmol/L but not for lower potassium (<4.0 mmol/L) After addition of serum albumin to the model , higher Sx but not lower Sx was associated with the composite outcome (Table 3) during"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Discussion","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":235.33333333333337,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":34.333333333333336,"r":97.0,"t":248.0},"charspan":[0,10],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/9","text":"Discussion"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"To the best   of our the   first study to investigate systematically the prevalence and mortality associated with SK levels that are obtained during routine clinical practice in large and diverse nondialysis prospective CKD is that SK level 24.0 mmol/L was associated with a higher risk for mortality and ESRD compared with Sx between 4.1 and 5.5 mmol/L. These observations are of clinical importance, because most physicians consider Sx values of 3.5 to 3.9 mmol/L to be \"within normal limits Our multivariable analyses show that the lowest risk for mortality was in the Sx range of 4.1 to 5.5 mmol/L and that mortality or ESRD risk was not significantly higher even at Sk levels of 5.5 to 5.9 mmol/L, a range of Sx values that almost always evokes clinical concern with or without intervention dietary adprescription of diuretic, discontinuation of potassium-sparing diuretics) This modest   level of hyperkalemia finding vice, loop seemed to be well tolerated in this patient population from the perspective of predicting mortality risk.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":42.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":33.0,"r":289.0,"t":235.33333333333337},"charspan":[0,934],"page_no":1},{"bbox":{"b":462.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":297.0,"r":552.6666666666666,"t":488.6666666666667},"charspan":[935,1040],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/10","text":"To the best   of our the   first study to investigate systematically the prevalence and mortality associated with SK levels that are obtained during routine clinical practice in large and diverse nondialysis prospective CKD is that SK level 24.0 mmol/L was associated with a higher risk for mortality and ESRD compared with Sx between 4.1 and 5.5 mmol/L. These observations are of clinical importance, because most physicians consider Sx values of 3.5 to 3.9 mmol/L to be \"within normal limits Our multivariable analyses show that the lowest risk for mortality was in the Sx range of 4.1 to 5.5 mmol/L and that mortality or ESRD risk was not significantly higher even at Sk levels of 5.5 to 5.9 mmol/L, a range of Sx values that almost always evokes clinical concern with or without intervention dietary adprescription of diuretic, discontinuation of potassium-sparing diuretics) This modest   level of hyperkalemia finding vice, loop seemed to be well tolerated in this patient population from the perspective of predicting mortality risk."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Not surprising   diuretic use was associated with lower Sx levels in this study. Non-potassium-sparing diuretic use has been linked with increased mortality and hospitalization that can likely be attributed to low SK levels (23). Several casecontrol studies   reported an increased risk for cardiac arrest among patients who received high dosages of thiazide diuretics. This risk was lowered when thiazide therapy was combined with potassium-sparing diuretics (24-26). Because diuretics are very commonly used for volume and BP control in the CKD population and hypokalemia is a frequent complication of this class of medications, these results suggest that clinicians need to monitor SK levels closely and consider potassium sparing diuretics and /or potassium supplements in patients who have CKD and are prone to hypokalemia; however, clin ical trials will need to be performed before formulating definusing","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":42.66666666666663,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":296.6666666666667,"r":553.3333333333334,"t":223.66666666666663},"charspan":[0,910],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/11","text":"Not surprising   diuretic use was associated with lower Sx levels in this study. Non-potassium-sparing diuretic use has been linked with increased mortality and hospitalization that can likely be attributed to low SK levels (23). Several casecontrol studies   reported an increased risk for cardiac arrest among patients who received high dosages of thiazide diuretics. This risk was lowered when thiazide therapy was combined with potassium-sparing diuretics (24-26). Because diuretics are very commonly used for volume and BP control in the CKD population and hypokalemia is a frequent complication of this class of medications, these results suggest that clinicians need to monitor SK levels closely and consider potassium sparing diuretics and /or potassium supplements in patients who have CKD and are prone to hypokalemia; however, clin ical trials will need to be performed before formulating definusing"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"The link between relatively normal or low-normal levels of Sk (3.5 to 4.0 mmol/L) and mortality has not been previously documented in the CKD population.  This is not altosurprising, however. Low Sk levels affect myocardial resting membrane potential, which increases the probability of ventricular arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death (11). kalemia can also predispose patients to developing diastolic dysfunction;   digoxin   toxicity , and insulin   resistance, all of (12-16) Dietary potassium depletion has been linked to the genesis of hypertension; and supplementation can improve BP control (17-19). Data from the cardiovascular literature suggest that serum potassium levels <4.0 mmol/L portend worse prognosis among those who history of heart failure or acute myocardial infarction (20,21). Current American Heart Association American of Cardiology  guidelines  recommend that serum potassium levels be maintained between 4.0 and 5.0 mmol/L in those with chronic heart failure (22). Our study provides observational evidence that necessitates similar guidelines for patients with CKD. finding gether Hypohave College","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":222.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":297.0,"r":553.6666666666666,"t":462.6666666666667},"charspan":[0,1126],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/12","text":"The link between relatively normal or low-normal levels of Sk (3.5 to 4.0 mmol/L) and mortality has not been previously documented in the CKD population.  This is not altosurprising, however. Low Sk levels affect myocardial resting membrane potential, which increases the probability of ventricular arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death (11). kalemia can also predispose patients to developing diastolic dysfunction;   digoxin   toxicity , and insulin   resistance, all of (12-16) Dietary potassium depletion has been linked to the genesis of hypertension; and supplementation can improve BP control (17-19). Data from the cardiovascular literature suggest that serum potassium levels <4.0 mmol/L portend worse prognosis among those who history of heart failure or acute myocardial infarction (20,21). Current American Heart Association American of Cardiology  guidelines  recommend that serum potassium levels be maintained between 4.0 and 5.0 mmol/L in those with chronic heart failure (22). Our study provides observational evidence that necessitates similar guidelines for patients with CKD. finding gether Hypohave College"}],"version":"1.10.0"}